description:
Foundation half of bd:JMAP-ayoz.41's workspace cookie-cut for RFC 8620 §5.3 maxObjectsInSet enforcement.

## Scope

Two additions to crate-jmap-server:

### 1. JmapBackend::max_objects_in_set default method

Add a default-impl method to the JmapBackend supertrait (crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs):

```rust
/// Maximum number of objects the server will accept in a single `/set` call
/// (RFC 8620 §5.3 `maxObjectsInSet`). Counts create + update + destroy entries.
///
/// Handlers MUST enforce this at the top of `handle_*_set` (typically via
/// [`crate::helpers::enforce_max_objects_in_set`]) so a single batched call
/// cannot drive O(M·N) work against an indexed store.
///
/// The default value `500` mirrors the testjig Session JSON advertised cap
/// and matches the common Fastmail / Cyrus IMAP server defaults. Backends
/// MAY override per account (Free vs Pro tier, multi-tenant SaaS, etc.);
/// the `caller`/`account_id` args are passed even though the default impl
/// ignores them so production backends can vary without an API break.
fn max_objects_in_set(
    &self,
    caller: &Self::CallerCtx,
    account_id: &jmap_types::Id,
) -> u64 {
    let _ = (caller, account_id);
    500
}
```

Why on the foundation trait rather than per-extension XxxLimits struct: every extension *Backend supertraits JmapBackend, so one default impl covers all 8 extensions without per-crate boilerplate. Workspace AGENTS.md "Backend caps and limits" struct pattern is preferred for new extensions but introducing 7 new XxxLimits structs (sharing/tasks/contacts/filenode/metadata don't have one) is a separate workspace-architectural decision out of scope here.

### 2. enforce_max_objects_in_set helper in helpers.rs

Add to crate-jmap-server/src/helpers.rs:

```rust
/// Enforce RFC 8620 §5.3 `maxObjectsInSet` cap at the top of a `/set` handler.
///
/// Counts entries in the wire `create` (object), `update` (object), and
/// `destroy` (array) arguments and returns `JmapError::limit("maxObjectsInSet")`
/// (which maps to HTTP 400 + wire `type: "limit"` per RFC 8620 §3.6.1) if
/// the total exceeds `max`.
///
/// Call at the top of every `handle_*_set` after `account_exists` but before
/// any per-entry processing, so a request carrying a megabyte of `/set` ops
/// is rejected before the handler touches the storage layer.
///
/// Non-object `create` / `update` and non-array `destroy` are tolerated (they
/// count as 0) — wire shape validation belongs to the per-handler argument
/// parsing.
pub fn enforce_max_objects_in_set(
    args: &serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>,
    max: u64,
) -> Result<(), JmapError> {
    let count = args
        .get("create")
        .and_then(|v| v.as_object())
        .map_or(0u64, |m| m.len() as u64)
        + args
            .get("update")
            .and_then(|v| v.as_object())
            .map_or(0u64, |m| m.len() as u64)
        + args
            .get("destroy")
            .and_then(|v| v.as_array())
            .map_or(0u64, |a| a.len() as u64);
    if count > max {
        return Err(JmapError::limit("maxObjectsInSet"));
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

Casting `usize` to `u64` via `as` is fine here because both values are bounded by serde_json's body parse and JMAP `maxSizeRequest`; on 32-bit targets `usize` fits in `u64` losslessly.

## Tests

In crate-jmap-server/src/helpers.rs `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`:

1. `enforce_max_objects_in_set_zero_args_is_ok` — empty args returns Ok.
2. `enforce_max_objects_in_set_at_limit_is_ok` — exact boundary (5 + 5 + 5 = 15 with max=15) returns Ok.
3. `enforce_max_objects_in_set_over_limit_returns_limit_error` — (5 + 5 + 6 = 16 with max=15) returns Err matching `JmapError::limit("maxObjectsInSet")`. Independent oracle: hand-built JmapError with error_type="limit" + description="maxObjectsInSet".
4. `enforce_max_objects_in_set_ignores_non_object_create` — `create: null` is tolerated (counts as 0).
5. `enforce_max_objects_in_set_ignores_non_array_destroy` — `destroy: "not-an-array"` is tolerated (counts as 0).
6. `enforce_max_objects_in_set_sums_all_three` — verifies the addition includes all three sources.

In crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`:

7. `jmap_backend_max_objects_in_set_default_is_500` — construct a minimal test impl of JmapBackend and assert default returns 500.

## Acceptance

- `cargo fmt --all` clean.
- `cargo clippy -p jmap-server -- -D warnings` clean.
- `cargo test -p jmap-server` passes including the 7 new tests.
- `RUSTDOCFLAGS="-D warnings" cargo doc -p jmap-server --no-deps` clean.
- `cargo test --workspace` still passes — every extension server inherits the default-500 cap. Existing tests that batch >500 /set entries (if any) need investigation; current tests are small-batch.

